    <div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <h1 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">District 17 Leadership Directory</h1>
        <p class="lead">Use this directory to contact the Committee on Special Education (CSE) or CPSE offices in NYC District 17. Remember that effective communication is KEY to ensuring your child receives the appropriate special education services. This directory, combined with the guidance below, will help you navigate the process.</p>
        
        <div class="contact-grid">
            
            <div class="contact-box">
                <h3>CSE 6 (Serving District 17)</h3>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📍 Walk-in Center:</strong><br>5619 Flatlands Ave, Brooklyn, NY 11234</div>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📞 Phone:</strong><br><a href="tel:718-968-6200">718-968-6200</a></div>
                <div class="contact-item"><strong>📧 Official Email:</strong><br><a href="mailto:N/A">Contact via NYC Schools Account</a></div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="alert-box" style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 20px; margin-top: 30px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107;">
            <strong>⚠️ Escalation Tip:</strong> If you do not receive a response within 48 hours, document your attempt and escalate to the NYC DOE Central Region Office. Keep all communication in writing (email is preferable) and document dates, times, and names of individuals you spoke with.
        </div>

        
<section class="protocol-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-clipboard-check"></i> Communication Protocol
    </h2>
    
    <div class="protocol-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">
        
        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">The "Written Notice" Rule</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Never rely on a phone call alone.</strong> In New York Special Education law, timelines (like the 60-day evaluation window mandated by 8 NYCRR §200.4(b)(1)(i)) only officially start when the district receives a request <em>in writing</em>. This is crucial for establishing a clear timeline for evaluations, IEP meetings, and service implementation.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Always follow up a phone call with an email summary, reiterating the key points discussed and any agreements made.</li>
                <li>Use the subject line: <em>"Letter of Referral - [Student Name]"</em> or <em>"IEP Meeting Request - [Student Name]"</em>.  Be specific!</li>
                <li>Keep a "Communication Log" of dates, times, names, and a brief summary of each interaction. This log is invaluable if you need to file a complaint.</li>
                <li>When sending emails, request a "read receipt" to confirm the email was opened. While not legally binding, it provides evidence that the email was likely received.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">When to Escalate?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                If you contact the leadership listed above and do not receive a response, follow this escalation ladder:
            </p>
            <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Initial Email/Phone Call. Clearly state your request or concern.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Follow-up Email (CC the Principal and the CSE Chairperson if known). Reiterate your initial request and reference your previous communication.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Escalation Email to the Superintendent or District Superintendent.  Include a concise summary of the situation, the lack of response, and the specific relief you are seeking (e.g., "I am requesting an immediate IEP meeting to address my child's lack of progress in reading.").</li>
                <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> File a "State Complaint" with NYSED if services are denied or the district fails to adhere to timelines outlined in Part 200 of the NYS Regulations (8 NYCRR Part 200).  You can find the complaint form on the NYSED website.  Be sure to include all relevant documentation, including your communication log.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="iep-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-file-contract"></i> Navigating the IEP Process in District 17
    </h2>

    <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">The Individualized Education Program (IEP) is the cornerstone of your child's special education journey. Understanding your rights and responsibilities within the IEP process is essential.</p>

    <div class="iep-steps" style="margin-top: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Key IEP Steps & Parent Advocacy Tips:</h3>
        <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
            <li>
                <strong>Referral:</strong>  Any concerned party (parent, teacher, doctor) can refer a child for a special education evaluation.  Document everything in writing. The school has a legal obligation to respond to your referral within a specific timeframe, outlined in 8 NYCRR §200.4(a)(1)(i). <br>
                <strong>Parent Tip:</strong> If you suspect your child needs special education services, don't hesitate to make a written referral. Early intervention is crucial.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Evaluation:</strong>  The school district must conduct a comprehensive evaluation in all areas of suspected disability (8 NYCRR §200.4(b)). This includes psychological, educational, and social assessments.  You have the right to an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) at public expense if you disagree with the school's evaluation, under specific circumstances (8 NYCRR §200.5(g)).
                 <br>
                <strong>Parent Tip:</strong>  Actively participate in the evaluation process. Provide the evaluators with your insights into your child's strengths and weaknesses. If you pursue an IEE, ensure the evaluator is qualified and experienced in assessing your child's specific needs.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>IEP Meeting:</strong>  The IEP meeting is where the team (including you, teachers, administrators, and related service providers) develops the IEP. The IEP must be based on the child's individual needs and must be designed to provide a Free Appropriate Public Education (FAPE) in the Least Restrictive Environment (LRE) as mandated by the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA).
                 <br>
                <strong>Parent Tip:</strong> Prepare for the IEP meeting by reviewing all evaluation reports and creating a list of your child's needs and goals. Don't be afraid to ask questions and advocate for your child's best interests. Bring a friend or advocate for support.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>IEP Implementation:</strong>  Once the IEP is finalized, the school district is legally obligated to implement it.  Monitor your child's progress closely and communicate regularly with the teachers and service providers.
                 <br>
                <strong>Parent Tip:</strong> Keep detailed records of your child's progress (or lack thereof). If the IEP is not being implemented correctly or is not effective, request an IEP meeting to revise it.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Annual Review:</strong>  The IEP must be reviewed at least annually (8 NYCRR §200.4(e)).  This is an opportunity to assess your child's progress, make necessary adjustments to the IEP, and plan for the future.
                 <br>
                <strong>Parent Tip:</strong> The annual review is not just a formality. Use it as a chance to thoroughly evaluate your child's needs and ensure the IEP continues to meet them.
            </li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-question-circle"></i> Frequently Asked Questions
    </h2>

    <div class="faq-items">
        <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #c8102e;">Q: What if I disagree with the IEP?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #444;">
                A: You have several options if you disagree with the IEP, including:
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Mediation:</strong> A neutral third party helps you and the school district reach an agreement.</li>
                    <li><strong>Impartial Hearing:</strong> A formal hearing where an impartial hearing officer makes a decision.  You have the right to present evidence and witnesses. (8 NYCRR §200.5(j))</li>
                    <li><strong>State Complaint:</strong> File a complaint with NYSED alleging that the school district violated IDEA or Part 200 regulations.</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #c8102e;">Q: What is the "Stay Put" provision?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #444;">
                A: The "Stay Put" provision (IDEA 20 U.S.C. § 1415(j)) states that during any dispute regarding your child's IEP, your child remains in their current educational placement unless you and the school district agree otherwise. This protects your child from changes to their services while you are resolving a disagreement.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #c8102e;">Q: My child is not making progress. What should I do?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #444;">
                A: First, document the lack of progress with specific examples. Then, request an IEP meeting to discuss your concerns. The IEP team should review the data, identify the reasons for the lack of progress, and make adjustments to the IEP. If the school is unresponsive, consider seeking an IEE or filing a complaint.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="additional-resources" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-book-open"></i> Additional Resources for District 17 Families
    </h2>
    <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li><strong>New York State Education Department (NYSED) Special Education Website:</strong> <a href="http://www.nysed.gov/specialed" target="_blank">www.nysed.gov/specialed</a> - Provides information on special education laws, regulations, and policies.</li>
        <li><strong>Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA):</strong> <a href="https://sites.ed.gov/idea/" target="_blank">https://sites.ed.gov/idea/</a> - The federal law that governs special education.</li>
        <li><strong>INCLUDEnyc:</strong> <a href="https://www.includenyc.org/" target="_blank">https://www.includenyc.org/</a> - A New York City-based organization that provides information, training, and advocacy services to families of children with disabilities.</li>
        <li><strong>Disability Rights New York (DRNY):</strong> <a href="https://www.drny.org/" target="_blank">https://www.drny.org/</a> - The protection and advocacy agency for people with disabilities in New York State.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

